            <ArticleTitle>Profile of coronary artery risk factors in Ethiopian diabetic patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>105-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the coronary artery risk factors in Ethiopian diabetic patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross sectional study done on a representative sample of diabetic patients to evaluate the coronary risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hospital-based study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 302 diabetic patients randomly selected from the 3000 regularly attending diabetic patients in the Tikur Anbessa Hospital Diabetes Centre of whom 161(53.3%) were males, 141(46.7%) were females, 140(46.4%) were Type 1 and 162(53.6%) were Type 2.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Assessment of the coronary risk factors in diabetic patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 20(6.6%) smokers and no ex-smokers. All smokers were males of whom five (25%) were Type 1 and 15(75%) were Type 2. Hypertension and obesity were found in 64(21.2%) and 69(22.8%) patients respectively. Sixty one (20.2%) patients and 43(14.2%) patients had hypercholesterolaemia and hypertriglyceridaemia respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our diabetic patients share the risk factors for developing coronary artery disease like any other diabetic patient in the developed countries but at a lower level.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
